From: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mfd: tps65219: log and clear POWER_UP_STATUS at probe (brownout diagnostics)

The board reboots in a loop on a long 5 V cable: a Joulescope on the feed
shows a 540 mA then an 800 mA pulse, 1 ms each, 12 ms apart, 4.12 s after
power-on - the panel's rail switching on and its reset releasing - and the
board resets right after the second one. What is not known is *what*
resets: the whole 5 V input dropping out (the PD controller / input switch
tripping on the dip), or the SoC alone.

The TPS65214's POWER_UP_STATUS_REG answers that: bit 0 (POWER_UP_FROM_OFF)
is set if VSYS fell below UVLO since the flags were last cleared, i.e. the
PMIC itself lost its input. It is write-1-to-clear. Userspace cannot read
it inside the loop (the reset lands at ~1.5 s into the kernel, systemd has
not started), so print it here at MFD probe (~0.4 s) and clear it, so that
each iteration of the loop reports what ended the previous one. Printed at
KERN_ERR only so it reaches the serial console at the image's console
loglevel of 4; it is a one-liner per boot.

  tps65219 0-0030: POWER_UP_STATUS=0x59 (VSYS lost since last clear: yes, retry=0)

"yes" every iteration means the 5 V input path is cutting out; "no" means
the PMIC stayed up and the reset came from elsewhere (3.3 V rail, SoC
monitor, external watchdog).

Signed-off-by: Eric Tanner <eric.tanner@harvia.com>
---
--- a/drivers/mfd/tps65219.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/tps65219.c
@@ -508,6 +508,15 @@ static int tps65219_probe(struct i2c_client *client)
 			dev_err(tps->dev, "Failed to find TPS65214\n");
 			return -ENODEV;
 		}
+
+		/* Brownout diagnostics: why did we power up? W1C flags. */
+		if (!regmap_read(tps->regmap, TPS65219_REG_POWER_UP_STATUS, &id)) {
+			dev_err(tps->dev,
+				"POWER_UP_STATUS=0x%02x (VSYS lost since last clear: %s, retry=%u)\n",
+				id, (id & BIT(0)) ? "yes" : "no", (id >> 1) & 3);
+			regmap_write(tps->regmap, TPS65219_REG_POWER_UP_STATUS,
+				     id & (BIT(7) | BIT(6) | BIT(5) | BIT(0)));
+		}
 	}
 
 	ret = devm_regmap_add_irq_chip(tps->dev, tps->regmap, client->irq,
